Subject: elisp packages that cannot be built as they stand
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.  Held until Thomas answers the first mail.  The
diffs below are pasted in from emacs-framework/pkg-fixes/; re-check
that they still apply on the day it goes out.)

Hi Thomas,

This is separate from the framework thread because it is true of
pkgsrc as it is today and does not depend on any of that.  Please say
if you would rather have it as a PR; I am happy to send it that way
instead.

Going through the packages that spell ${PKGBASE} in their own Makefile
turned up five where the install writes ${PKGBASE} while the PLIST, or
the install itself, spells the bare name.  The two agree only while
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX is empty, and it is not empty for xemacs.  I
built an xemacs-nox11 21.5.36 here and ran the five against it, with
your modules.mk and the packages untouched, so what follows is
measured rather than read.

Three fail straight away, each in a different place:

  devel/ecb            stops before it builds anything.  WRKSRC comes
                       out as work/xemacs-ecb-<tag>, because
                       mk/fetch/github.mk has GITHUB_PROJECT?=
                       ${PKGBASE}, and the archive unpacks as
                       ecb-<tag>:
                         cd: Can't cd to .../work/xemacs-ecb-1330a44...

  editors/gnuserv      INSTALLATION_DIRS makes share/doc/${PKGBASE},
                       which is share/doc/xemacs-gnuserv, while
                       doc-install writes to share/doc/gnuserv, which
                       does not exist -- so that name becomes a
                       484-byte file holding whichever README was
                       installed last, the empty directory sits beside
                       it, and the file check stops the build.

  textproc/dictem      installs share/doc/xemacs-dictem/{COPYING,NEWS,
                       README} while the PLIST asks for
                       share/doc/dictem/*.

editors/matlab-mode and textproc/xslide have the same shape in their
Makefiles, and they need two fixes each, because the first failure
hides the second.  They stop at the byte-compile:

  matlab-mode  Cannot open load file: "tempo"
  xslide       Cannot open load file: "sendmail"

Neither library is in XEmacs; both are in editors/xemacs-packages, and
neither package depends on it.  devel/doxymacs and textproc/migemo-elisp
already carry that dependency in their xemacs branch, so the missing
line has a precedent to copy.  With it added they get past the compile
and land on the ${PKGBASE} problem after all:

  matlab-mode  creates .../lisp/matlab-mode, installs into
               .../lisp/xemacs-matlab-mode
               install: the last argument ... must name an
               existing directory
  xslide       PLIST .../lisp/xslide, destdir .../lisp/xemacs-xslide

With both fixes they build: xemacs-matlab-mode-2.3.1nb4 and
xemacs-xslide-0.2.2nb4.

devel/ecb I could not get to build at all, so there is no patch for it
below -- what follows is what I found rather than a fix.  Adding
GITHUB_PROJECT= ecb does get it past the fetch, and it then stops in
the byte-compile:

  Symbol's function definition is void: semantic-mode

ecb-2.50's own Makefile writes (semantic-mode 1) into the compile
script, and XEmacs does not have that function -- with
xemacs-nox11-21.5.36nb1 and xemacs-packages-1.19nb1 installed,
(fboundp 'semantic-mode) is nil both bare and after (require
'semantic).  Since the package takes xemacs215, xemacs215nox,
xemacs214 and xemacs214nox and nothing else, there is no Emacs it
accepts that can compile it.

While looking I noticed editors/xemacs-packages already installs ECB,
63 files under lib/xemacs/xemacs-packages/lisp/ecb, so XEmacs users
have one either way.  Whether devel/ecb should follow ECB upstream,
depend on the bundled one, or be marked is not mine to decide, which
is the other reason there is no diff.

cad/dinotrace-mode spells ${PKGBASE} too and is fine.  It is the only
one of the nine whose PLIST also uses it, so both sides move together,
which is the whole distinction.

While installing xemacs I ran into something else.  editors/gnuserv
has

  CONFLICTS+=	xemacs-[0-9]* xemacs-current-[0-9]* # bin/gnuattach; bin/gnuclient

and neither pattern does what it looks like.  The packages are named
xemacs-21.4.25, xemacs-nox11-21.4.25, xemacs-21.5.36 and
xemacs-nox11-21.5.36, so xemacs-current-[0-9]* matches nothing at all,
and xemacs-[0-9]* does not cover the nox ones:

  pkg_info -e 'xemacs-[0-9]*'          rc=1
  pkg_info -e 'xemacs-current-[0-9]*'  rc=1
  pkg_info -e 'xemacs-nox11-[0-9]*'    rc=0  xemacs-nox11-21.5.36nb1

I found it by having it happen: pkg_add refused xemacs-nox11 over
bin/gnuattach with gnuserv already installed, which is the collision
the line is there to declare.  The diff below makes it
xemacs-nox11-[0-9]*.

mail/mu is broken for a different reason, and this one I did build.
mail/mu cannot be packaged against an Emacs without D-Bus -- every nox
one, and an emacs30 with the dbus option turned off:

  pkg_create: can't stat .../site-lisp/mu4e/mu4e-dbus.elc

mu4e/meson.build asks the Emacs whether dbusbind is compiled in and
only byte-compiles mu4e-dbus.el when it is, while PLIST.emacs lists
the .elc with no condition.  The guard turns out not to be needed:
both requires in mu4e-dbus.el pass 'noerror, and with
emacs30-nox11-30.2nb1, which has no dbusbind, the file byte-compiles
with no output at all and the .elc loads.  With the guard removed,
mail/mu builds and the package has both mu4e-dbus.el and
mu4e-dbus.elc.

One more, which is recent rather than ancient.  misc/elscreen cannot
be built at all:

  misc/elscreen/Makefile 1.28   EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED= emacs20
                                .include ".../devel/apel/buildlink3.mk"
  devel/apel/Makefile    1.46   EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED+= emacs29 ...

Its only accepted Emacs is one its only elisp dependency refuses, and
the include is at top level rather than inside a condition.  The
dependency is real: elscreen.el requires alist and static, both apel.

I first read this as fallout from 2026-09-12 because both files carry
that date, but it is older.  In a 2 September copy apel is already at
1.45, from 26 August, with the same emacs29-and-up list, while
elscreen still accepted emacs21 emacs21nox emacs20.  So the overlap
went on 26 August, and the September commit only took emacs21 out of a
list that had already stopped working.  Its own comment says "Emacs 24
or later is not supported.  Try wip/elscreen-current instead", and
apel names emacs20 and emacs21 in EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE, so
there is no version where the two can meet.  I have not written a
patch for that one: whether elscreen should follow wip/elscreen-current,
or be marked, is not mine to decide.

Three others reach apel the same way and are only partly affected,
because apel's buildlink3.mk opens with .if ${EMACS_FLAVOR} ==
"emacs", so under xemacs the dependency is simply not there:

  misc/lookup              emacs20 fails; xemacs214/215 are fine
  www/emacs-w3m            emacs20 fails; emacs29 and up are fine
  www/emacs-w3m-snapshot   the same

That flavour guard is worth knowing about before counting this sort of
thing: editors/mule-ucs guards its buildlink with .if ${EMACS_FLAVOR}
== "emacs" && ${EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR} < 22, so a plain read of who
depends on whom reports pairs that do not exist at the version being
built.

While I was in these Makefiles I went looking for declarations that no
longer describe anything, since that is the kind of thing that rots
quietly.  Two sorts turned up.

devel/semi is the serious one.  Its options.mk still has

  .if !empty(PKG_OPTIONS:Msemi-bbdb)
  .include "../../misc/bbdb2/buildlink3.mk"

and misc/bbdb2 went out with emacs21, so with that option set the
package does not fail to build -- it fails to parse:

  bmake: ".../devel/semi/options.mk" line 14:
         Could not find ../../misc/bbdb2/buildlink3.mk
  bmake: Fatal errors encountered -- cannot continue

misc/bbdb3 is still there, so the obvious repair is to point the
option at it, and that is what I tried first.  It builds -- and it
would not work.  mime-bbdb.el is BBDB 2 code, and against bbdb3 it
byte-compiles with

  bbdb-record-getprop, bbdb-record-putprop, bbdb-search-simple,
  bbdb-pop-up-bbdb-buffer, bbdb-record-edit-notes, bbdb-annotate-notes

all "not known to be defined".  I went through bbdb-3.2.2a's lisp and
none of the six is there under any of those names, so the package
would install a file that fails the moment it is used.

So the diff below takes the option out instead, with the reasoning in
a comment where the block was.  devel/semi then builds, and setting
PKG_OPTIONS.semi=semi-bbdb no longer does anything rather than
stopping make.  If someone wants the feature back, what is missing is
a port of mime-bbdb.el to BBDB 3, not a line in options.mk.  I left
PLIST_VARS and the PLIST entry alone; with the option gone the line is
simply never emitted.

The other sort is CONFLICTS lines that match no package.  I checked
each with pkg_admin pmatch rather than by eye, which was worth doing
because one I was sure about turned out to be fine (mail/elm's PKGNAME
is elm-2.5.8nb5, not the elm2.5.8 its DISTNAME suggests).

  devel/pcl-cvs      CONFLICTS= emacs-21.*
                     emacs21's PKGNAME was emacs21-21.4anb49, which
                     that never matched; and it is gone now anyway
  inputmethod/skk    CONFLICTS= ddskk-[0-9]*        no such package
  mail/mew           CONFLICTS+= mew-xemacs-[0-9]*  no such package
  inputmethod/       CONFLICTS= tamago-anthy-[0-9]*
    tamago-tsunagi   only in wip

None of these breaks anything today -- a CONFLICTS that matches
nothing simply does nothing.  They are worth a look because the thing
they were guarding may still be true.  editors/gnuserv above is the
case where it was.

There is one that goes the other way -- it matches, and I do not think
it should.  misc/bbdb3 has

  CONFLICTS+=		xemacs-packages-[0-9]*

and it stopped a build of mine: mail/wl-snapshot wants bbdb, and
pkg_add refused emacs30nox-bbdb-3.2.2anb3 because xemacs-packages was
installed.  bbdb3 takes emacs29, emacs30 and emacs31 and no xemacs at
all, so it cannot produce a file under lib/xemacs; I compared the two
packing lists to be sure rather than reasoning about it, and of
bbdb3's 38 files and xemacs-packages' 7840 not one name is shared.
I have not put a diff for it below, because I would rather ask: is
this line left over from when bbdb3 accepted xemacs, or is there a
collision I am not seeing?  If it is the former I am happy to send the
one-line removal.

Everything below was built with the diff in place, on NetBSD
11.0/amd64, against xemacs-nox11-21.5.36nb1 and xemacs-packages-1.19nb1
where the package takes XEmacs and emacs30-nox11-30.2nb1 where it takes
Emacs:

  xemacs-gnuserv-3.12.8        xemacs-matlab-mode-2.3.1nb4
  xemacs-dictem-1.0.4nb3       xemacs-xslide-0.2.2nb4
  semi-1.14.6nb8               mu-1.14.3

For mail/mu I also opened the package: it has both mu4e-dbus.el and
mu4e-dbus.elc, this Emacs has (featurep 'dbusbind) nil, and the .elc
loads with the mu4e directory on load-path.

The diffs are below.  I checked them in check mode against trunk as of
2026-09-17: they apply with no offset and no fuzz, in the order given
and reversed.
For mail/mu the file patches/patch-mu4e_meson.build already exists, so
that one is a change to an existing patch rather than a new one, and
its distinfo line moves with it.

Showta

--- editors/gnuserv/Makefile.orig
+++ editors/gnuserv/Makefile
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@
 HOMEPAGE=	https://www.emacswiki.org/emacs/GnuClient
 COMMENT=	Improved version of emacsserver for GNU Emacs
 
-CONFLICTS+=	xemacs-[0-9]* xemacs-current-[0-9]* # bin/gnuattach; bin/gnuclient
+CONFLICTS+=	xemacs-[0-9]* xemacs-nox11-[0-9]* # bin/gnuattach; bin/gnuclient
 GNU_CONFIGURE=	YES
 
 CONFIGURE_ARGS+=	--with-x	# X support
@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@
 EL_FILES=		devices.el gnuserv.el gnuserv-compat.el
 
 INSTALLATION_DIRS+=	${EMACS_LISPPREFIX} share/applications	\
-			share/doc/${PKGBASE}
+			share/doc/gnuserv
 
 .PHONY: el-install doc-install
 

--- textproc/dictem/Makefile.orig
+++ textproc/dictem/Makefile
@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@
 SRCS=			dictem.el
 DOCS=			README NEWS COPYING # ChangeLog
 
-INSTALLATION_DIRS+=	share/doc/${PKGBASE} ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}
+INSTALLATION_DIRS+=	share/doc/dictem ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}
 
 do-build:
 .for f in ${SRCS}
@@ -29,7 +29,7 @@
 	cd ${WRKSRC} && ${INSTALL_DATA} ${SRCS} ${SRCS:.el=.elc}	\
 			${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX};		\
 	cd ${WRKSRC} && ${INSTALL_DATA} ${DOCS}				\
-			${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/doc/${PKGBASE}
+			${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/doc/dictem
 
 .include "../../editors/emacs/modules.mk"
 .include "../../mk/bsd.pkg.mk"

--- editors/matlab-mode/Makefile.orig
+++ editors/matlab-mode/Makefile
@@ -23,6 +23,9 @@
 EMACS=	emacs
 .else
 EMACS=	xemacs
+# matlab.el requires tempo, which lives in xemacs-packages rather
+# than in XEmacs itself, so the byte-compile below cannot find it.
+DEPENDS+=	xemacs-packages-[0-9]*:../../editors/xemacs-packages
 .endif
 
 SRCS=	matlab.el
@@ -34,7 +37,7 @@
 	done
 
 do-install:
-	${INSTALL_DATA_DIR} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^xemacs-||}
-	cd ${WRKSRC} && ${INSTALL_DATA} ${SRCS} ${SRCS:.el=.elc} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE}
+	${INSTALL_DATA_DIR} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}||}
+	cd ${WRKSRC} && ${INSTALL_DATA} ${SRCS} ${SRCS:.el=.elc} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}||}
 
 .include "../../mk/bsd.pkg.mk"

--- textproc/xslide/Makefile.orig
+++ textproc/xslide/Makefile
@@ -17,6 +17,9 @@
 EMACS=	emacs
 .else
 EMACS=	xemacs
+# The byte-compile below loads sendmail, which lives in
+# xemacs-packages rather than in XEmacs itself.
+DEPENDS+=	xemacs-packages-[0-9]*:../../editors/xemacs-packages
 .endif
 
 SRCS=	xslide-abbrev.el xslide-data.el xslide-font.el xslide-process.el xslide.el
@@ -32,8 +35,8 @@
 	done
 
 do-install:
-	${INSTALL_DATA_DIR} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE}
-	cd ${WRKSRC} && ${INSTALL_DATA} ${SRCS} ${SRCS:.el=.elc} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE}
+	${INSTALL_DATA_DIR} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}||}
+	cd ${WRKSRC} && ${INSTALL_DATA} ${SRCS} ${SRCS:.el=.elc} ${DESTDIR}${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}||}
 	${INSTALL_DATA_DIR} ${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/doc/xslide
 	${INSTALL_DATA} ${WRKSRC}/README.TXT ${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/doc/xslide
 	${INSTALL_DATA} ${WRKSRC}/dot_emacs ${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/doc/xslide

--- mail/mu/patches/patch-mu4e_meson.build.orig
+++ mail/mu/patches/patch-mu4e_meson.build
@@ -2,9 +2,33 @@
 
 Don't care about info errors
 
+Byte-compile mu4e-dbus.el unconditionally.  The build asks the Emacs whether
+dbusbind is compiled in and skips the file when it is not, but PLIST.emacs
+lists mu4e-dbus.elc with no condition, so the package cannot be built against
+an Emacs without D-Bus -- every *nox one, and an emacs30 with the dbus option
+off.  The file compiles clean without D-Bus: both of its requires pass
+'noerror.
+
 --- mu4e/meson.build.orig	2026-08-09 13:09:56.075658158 +0000
 +++ mu4e/meson.build
-@@ -156,6 +156,7 @@ if makeinfo.found()
+@@ -80,13 +80,9 @@
+ 
+ # do have we have dbus support?
+ mu4e_srcs += 'mu4e-dbus.el'
+-emacs_dbus = run_command(emacs, '--batch', '--eval', '(kill-emacs (if (featurep \'dbusbind) 0 1))',
+-                         check: false)
+-if emacs_dbus.returncode() == 0
+-  mu4e_bc_srcs += 'mu4e-dbus.el'
+-else
+-  message('mu4e-dbus.el cannot be byte-compiled')
+-endif
++# Byte-compile this whether or not the Emacs has dbusbind; both of its
++# requires are 'noerror, so it compiles without D-Bus.
++mu4e_bc_srcs += 'mu4e-dbus.el'
+ 
+ # note, we cannot compile mu4e-config.el without incurring
+ #  WARNING: Source item
+@@ -156,6 +152,7 @@ if makeinfo.found()
                  install: true,
                  depends: fdl_texi,
                  command: [makeinfo,

--- mail/mu/distinfo.orig
+++ mail/mu/distinfo
@@ -4,4 +4,4 @@
 SHA512 (mu-1.14.3.tar.gz) = 7ac28e81f646a9bdd0dd1140d7d0517b8a83eb4383c2a2e1b9e44ca1b1130e408c6db15da85b73f0ffd894a1b449bdb70a896fbd7527d79a843b42c4ae4b80fa
 Size (mu-1.14.3.tar.gz) = 1338427 bytes
 SHA1 (patch-lib_message_mu-contact.hh) = cc4cc391ab4bbd16f6c38e222057b392d68485e6
-SHA1 (patch-mu4e_meson.build) = 3581ab81c473ae058869b90e979fc77c96cf0670
+SHA1 (patch-mu4e_meson.build) = 7119708a5472bcc1b048a900e2db3b059e84aa0f

--- devel/semi/options.mk.orig
+++ devel/semi/options.mk
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 # $NetBSD: options.mk,v 1.7 2022/06/05 02:39:18 mef Exp $
 
 PKG_OPTIONS_VAR=		PKG_OPTIONS.semi
-PKG_SUPPORTED_OPTIONS=		semi-bbdb
+PKG_SUPPORTED_OPTIONS=		# empty
 PKG_OPTIONS_OPTIONAL_GROUPS=	semi-browser
 PKG_OPTIONS_GROUP.semi-browser=	w3 w3m
 PKG_SUGGESTED_OPTIONS=
@@ -10,10 +10,14 @@
 
 PLIST_VARS+=	bbdb w3
 
-.if !empty(PKG_OPTIONS:Msemi-bbdb)
-.include "../../misc/bbdb2/buildlink3.mk"
-PLIST.bbdb=	yes
-.endif
+# The semi-bbdb option included misc/bbdb2, which went away with
+# emacs21.  Pointing it at misc/bbdb3 does not work: mime-bbdb.el is
+# written against BBDB 2 and byte-compiles with bbdb-record-getprop,
+# bbdb-record-putprop, bbdb-search-simple, bbdb-pop-up-bbdb-buffer,
+# bbdb-record-edit-notes and bbdb-annotate-notes all undefined, none
+# of which BBDB 3 defines.  Without a port of mime-bbdb.el there is
+# nothing for the option to select, and leaving it in makes the
+# package fail to parse rather than fail to build.
 
 .if !empty(PKG_OPTIONS:Mw3)
 .include "../../www/w3/buildlink3.mk"

